>> title: Hambāgu
>> category: Fried & Crispy
>> tags: japanese, beef, pork, panko, pan-fried, weeknight, family
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: ハンバーグ, hanbāgu, hambagu, hanbagu, ハンバーグステーキ, hamburg steak, Japanese hamburg steak, salisbury steak
>> pairs-with: gohan, kabocha-no-nimono
>> servings: 4
>> time: 50 min
>> slack: narrow — the dimple and the lid are the whole mechanism for keeping the juice inside; a patty left past its steam squeezes out into the pan and what is left is a dry meat biscuit, which the sauce made from that same juice cannot disguise
>> step.1: soak 5 min to a paste
>> step.2: sweat 6 min, then cool
>> step.3: knead 3 min, shape and dimple
>> step.4: sear, then lid on 8 min
>> step.5: reduce 3 min into the fond

Soak @panko{1/2%cup}(30 g) in @whole milk{3%Tbs}(45 mL) in a #bowl{} ~soak{5%min}, until it has gone to a loose paste with no dry crumbs left in it. This is not a filler — it is what holds the water in the patty.

Sweat @onion{1}(chopped fine) in @unsalted butter{1%Tbs}(15 g) in a #frying pan{} ~sweat{6%min} until soft and not coloured, then spread it on a cold plate to ~cool{10%min}. Warm onion melts the fat in the mince, and mince with melted fat in it crumbles instead of binding.

Knead @ground beef{12%oz}(340 g), @ground pork{5%oz}(150 g), @egg{1}, @kosher salt{1%tsp}, @black pepper{1/4%tsp} and @nutmeg{1/8%tsp} into @&(1)soaked panko{} together with @&(~1)cooled onion{} ~knead{3%min}, until the mix turns tacky and pale and holds a thumbprint. Shape four ovals, slap each one between your palms to knock the air out, and press a deep dimple into the middle so the centre does not dome and split.

Sear @&(~1)patties{} in @neutral oil{1%Tbs}(15 mL) ~sear{6%min}, three minutes a side, then splash @sake{2%Tbs}(30 mL) into the pan, clamp the lid on and ~steam{8%min} over low heat. A skewer through the middle should run clear.

Lift the patties out and reduce @red wine{1/4%cup}(60 mL), @ketchup{3%Tbs}(45 mL) and @Worcestershire sauce{1%Tbs}(15 mL) into @&(~1)pan juices{} ~reduce{3%min}, scraping the brown off the bottom, then pour it back over.
